ne up  artificially high and  the informants is a movie about an  insider at adm who basically whistle  blew to the sec about this price fixing  and at the time the fine that was given  kind of this is a short part over here  in the department of justice was the  highest fine ever given by the sec  to a company  for price fixing  so um  lysine itself like i said it's a  valuable commodity if you drive through  decatur illinois you can spell the  lysine tanks you can see the lysine  tanks they're impressive 600 000 liter  tanks that are are pumping out this  small amino acid  and then feeding it to  pigs and chicken as part of the  um you know way to to get them up to the  the growth that the things can go into  our food production  the lysine pathway itself is is very  straightforward there's 19 core genes in  the pathway uh 26 transcription factors  kind of on the left here you can see uh  that core pathway all the the genes  involved if you just took out all the  metabolism and drew straight lines from  gina gene that's what the pathway would  look like  and so  um we wanted to do a proof of  proof of concept showing that this  combination of both rational and  a-rational engineering of looking  broadly could have an impact and and  quickly make progress to improve strains  uh that we're making valuable chemicals  like lysine or valuable molecules like  lysine and so the approach that we took  is knowing those core 19 pathways we  looked across the entire genome we  designed uh libraries on the  onyx platform using the software that  combined i guess what you'd call  semi-rational ideas so you went through  the genome and you knocked out that's  what ko stands for here in this kind of  circle you design  made designs that knock up every single  gene in e coli independently of whether  you thought it hit the lysine pathway or  not and i call this kind of a  rational a-rational because  rationally you're making knockouts but  irrationally you're looking at  everything at once  on top of that we looked at uh you know  kind of the core uh genes in e coli  and uh looked at every single gene so  the ones that were not absolutely  essential so 3676 genes and we took the  promoter the driver in front of every  one of those genes and replaced it with  a novel promoter of varying strength so  what you can see on the right here is a  promoter ladder so these are our  promoter sequences that are of varying  strength so the idea is if you put this  sequence in front of a gene it will  have some effect on that gene either  make that gene express higher or make  that gene express lower and what you can  see here is we picked five different  strengths of promoters out of this  library provided by igem  and we rationally engineered but then a  rationally across every gene  one of these promoters in front of  every single gene so we have five  different promoter strengths in front of  every single gene and then we screened  it  so rationally we put a promoter in front  of your gene rationally knocked out  everything but we also a-rationally  looked at everything at once  and this is what the outcome is we  looked at 18 000 isolates  and then we looked at okay the mean here  is shown as zero  and we look for those things that really  had an impact either increase the amount  of lysine coming out or decrease the  amount of lysine coming out sometimes  you learn things that have negative  effects  and so looking there at that kind of of  space you can see that there's a number  of things in the far left in that  knockout collection that improve  production of lysine there's a number of  things in this what we call the pathway  so these are the core genes of the  pathway those 19 genes where we either  did promoter swaps put different  promoters in front of it did a small  saturation library where we changed the  amino acid that was already on on a  particular  gene in that pathway to a arginine or a  histidine or asparagine  or we did the promoter swap shown here  on the far right we took those libraries  that had a total of 200 000 designs we  screened 18 000 of them on a rapid fire  mass spec that took seven seconds per  sample  and in that space we found some really  interesting things so for example one of  the genes that's really key to this  pathway is called dap a  uh it's feedback inhibited by lysine  so we looked at this one saying okay  what's that feedback inhibition let's  look at every single amino acid on this  enzyme and let's change it let's do site  direct immunogenesis and change it to  every one of the other 19 amino acids  that possibly could be it's also nice we  had a control built in here so one of  these amino acids e84 we could change to  a threonine  tyrosine sorry uh threonine anyway get  my get my amino acids mixed up here  but this is a validated hit that was in  in the literature and  in this case we knew that if this one  came up we were going to get an  improvement in the light scene  um uh hit here so we we had something  internally we knew that this would come  up in our design library  and so we made progress so  going here to a background and these are  color coded by the various uh kind of  engineering that was done so you start  over on the left hand side with wild  type and this is the amount of lysine  produced  by wild type the red bar at the top of  the graph shows where this  you know e84 to tyrosine  mutation is  and that's that's what we want to get to  so that's the baseline so the wild type  actually well it produces below that  line and if we just looked in the  background at either genome-wide  knockouts or genome-wide promoter spots  thrown in blue and green here we could  find that there are certain genes that  we could hit  looking in this background of this  already improved variant that would  actually push our  production of lysine up even higher  we then started stacking those heads to  combine those hits so you start out with  e84t and all of a sudde