s is protein production  how much protein you can make this has  to do with protein function  enzymology if you will  in this case one of our collaborators  over at vib in in europe had four key  genes that were involved in  membrane generation so these are the  four genes shown here in the purple red  and orange boxes  and  she wanted to explore changing every  single amino acid to every other  possible amino acid across these four  proteins  and what's really cool is with the onyx  system this was not a labor-intensive uh  process this didn't take very long she  uploaded her genome picked her four  genes and said do substitutions click  the button uh and then it generated the  the various edits so in the case of lpxa  for example all 527 edits were generated  at a click of a button  and what you show over here on the right  is is a heat map so at the bottom is the  the residue which position which amino  acid position on the left hand side is  the the single letter code for every  single amino acid that could be  substituted in this case and if you look  very carefully at the top you see these  little bitty dots  these little bitty dots from all the  work that have been done for for many  many years in these four proteins these  little bitty dots are the residues that  were shown to be  uh important and vital  in membrane generation  across these four genes and what's  amazing to me is it's a relatively small  set competing considering the  combinatorial space of these four genes  and all the amino acids you could make  and then when you look at the heat map  what you can see is if you look in the  horizontal direction you see these kind  of patches of white and if you look  closely you'll see for example proline  is a very  uh poorly tolerated substitution so you  kind of see a horizontal line at the p  there saying that hey amino acid  substitution of proline at every one of  the amino acids isn't a good choice to  make  but you looked at every single amino  acid that's not always the case now if  you look at the at the  vertical line what you can see is that  we're able to identify essentially  residues that are critical um and that's  this basically if you look uh from top  to bottom or bottom to top  if you see a basically a white line this  means that this is a residue that is  critical  that is important it does not tolerate  substitution very well so these are  other key residues that you might want  to go after to understand the  functionality of this enzyme  and so  you know this is a much fuller map a  much more detailed map on the enzyme  space that you can explore uh things  that you can go after things that you  can substitute for uh things that you  don't want to do but it's a  comprehensive map that allows you to  go on to the the next  national approach to improve these  enzymes  and then finally one final example  and this is the idea of  taking a traditional methodology and  doing what they call decompolation  finding out the the key changes that  might have happened in a change over  time so  uh there's a process called adaptive  laboratory evolution so this is the idea  that you want a a strain or a cell to be  more tolerant to some stress say  heat or ph or in this case acid  tolerance  so over the years uh through various  literature-based methodologies there are  992  e coli strains that have been generated  uh that shows some sort of acid  tolerance  uh  you know in these 992 strains there are  372 identified variants  168 transposon insertions and 542  knockouts all that for some reason or  another  give you a phenotype that makes you acid  tolerant  uh so what our collaborator did is it  took all 992 of these changes and said  okay which ones are are really important  which ones can really give me the best  acid tolerance  and he designed those as part of the  inscriptive platform so this is informed  by a rational mutagenesis but now it's a  rational library and he was able to take  all 992 of those unique elements put  them into a single library expose them  to this case chameric acid and say okay  which one make my cells more resistant  which one make them less resistant and  find genes that are are really key  again you want to look at that that far  upper right con quadrant say what are  those things that  truly make me the most acid tolerant and  then you can then map those residues of  those genes that change in this case  looking at rna polymerase subunit alpha  so rpoa  and say okay  these green residues and the in the  crystal structure make the cell uh make  the the protein make the the cells more  tolerant to acid  those in red or critical they make it  less tolerant  so  it's a way to to take you know what  would be a really really hard to do a b  testing across 992  unique snips or unique changes it's a  way to do it all at once and let your  winners rise to the top and uh make  progress and and back towards either  basic science or to the production of a  of a protein or a cluster of proteins  here that are important to uh conferring  acid tolerance which is important at  scale  so those are my examples i think we have  some some time left for questions i'm  going to go ahead  and pause here and let the moderator  come back and and let me know if there's  any questions i can answer for anybody  who listens  thank you patrick for your informative  presentation we will now start the live  q a portion of the webinar if you have a  question you'd like to ask please do so  now just click on the ask a question box  located on the far left of your screen  we'll answer as many of your questions  as we have time for  all right let's get started our first  question asks  one of the limitations of empirical  engineering is an appropriate screening  method often we are stuck with  growth-based assays is this instrument  capable of screening for  non-growth-based phenotypes such as  production and if so  how  what screen does it use cytometry  perhaps right