tion  produces more lysine  on the other hand if you're doing a  selection what you can do is take that  population of cells and expose it to uh  you know some sort of stress or some  sort of uh  something over time  and  what happens is in that case that you  can then  look and see okay which  uh phenotypes and and then relate that  back to the barcode get enriched so for  example let's say you want to look at uh  growing on glycerol so you could take  your yeast cell that has this massive  generated library you could grow it up  in glycerol and then after two days of  growing on glycerol go and say okay what  bar codes got enriched what barcodes are  now more present which barcodes fell out  which which designs spell out that are  no longer compatible with growth on  glycerol  so knowing if you're going to do a  screen or knowing if you're going to do  a selection does maybe change the types  of things that you uh would would  engineer in this approach i still think  the empirical methods are applicable but  maybe your rational methods may change  if you're in that screening versus  selection regime and looking at your  capacity to  find positive hits or winners if you  will  great thank you this next one has three  parts to it and it asks what is the  range of species in the onyx platform  compatible with what are the limitations  of the onyx platform in terms of species  compatibility and lastly what  characteristics does a species need to  have to be compatible with the onyx  platform  right so the onyx platform has its  release now is compatible with e coli  and saccharomyces although we are  looking to expand that range to other  organisms of industrial relevance  in the case of e coli we have two  standard strains called one is called uh  k strain uh also known as mg 1655 the  other one is is what we call b strains  this is the kind of uh protein  expression strain strains where  proteases are are knocked out  on the saccharomyces side we have two  strains one is s28c which is the kind of  standard academic laboratory strain of  saccharomyces the other one is senpique  and some variants the pk2 or cpk113d  both those cases the snpk2 strains are  considered to be more relevant to  industrial scale  and so there's a number of products made  by  companies that use this npk2 string  so those initial strains that we are are  supporting with that being said one of  the tools that script has developed is  what we call a custom strain assessment  library so this is a  library of about uh 900 edits  that are part of the um that we provide  to our customers and we allow them to  take their strain their e coli or their  saccharomyces strain that is not one of  our base strains uh deploy this library  against it and then look at the iterate  and see if that that the technology has  it exists is compatible with their  strains so this custom strain assessment  is a really important key part of making  sure that the onyx platform and the onyx  technology is compatible with um the  work that you are doing and with your  strain that you are trying to to work  with  great thank you this next question asks  how would you generate a combinatorial  library so do an expression optimize a  pathway of several enzymes  yeah so let's say you have  uh an example of a pathway that you know  it could either be a native pathway or a  heterologous pathway  it's a very simple process of you upload  your genome that includes that pathway  into the inscriptor designer software um  and in that script designer software you  then go to your you know those key genes  that are mapped and really easy to find  within the software  and let's say you want to do saturation  geogenesis you then take that gene and  you have a choice you can either pick  out individual residues and there's a  drop down menu that says what do you  want to do do you want to do a deletion  here you want to do a substitution you  want to do a change  and through that you can you can  b